  "note": "Figure 2.1 on the facing page is the commuting diagram this replaces: 12 polygons, 9 of them naturality squares. The page draws the A wire as one straight 4:1 diagonal leaving through the figure's left and right EDGES rather than its bottom, so the two beads riding it — σ where it meets C, λ where it meets B — are the whole content, and the diagonal is what the book's next sentence tells you to drag. The page prints μ on BOTH merges, at B and at C; our signature table is keyed by name, so the second one is called ν here and is the only label that differs from the page. The page's two μ beads sit at ONE height (y 404.28 for both); ours stacks them, which the port graph does not see. The painted region is the book's yellow, not the grey `𝟏` strip, so the object wire and its strip are ours alone — the declared `object-wire-added`. The right-hand panel is the same 2-cell after the two distributive laws are applied twice: τ still first, then the A wire crosses C, C, B, B on its way down the diagonal, and the two μ merges come last.",
